Simple Laceration Repair and Suture/Staple Removal
General Practice Other
Superficial linear skin defect, Absence of deep structural damage, Primary closure/suture removal, Well-approximated wound edges

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-041
Reason for Visit: Acute, actively bleeding laceration sustained during a domestic fall requiring immediate primary closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient who sustained a deep, gaping laceration to the forearm following a fall against a glass table, with continuous bleeding despite initial first aid. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to achieve definitive hemostasis, rule out underlying tendon or nerve damage, and perform an immediate surgical repair under local anesthesia to prevent wound contamination and tissue necrosis overnight. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of significant blood loss, infection, and irreversible cosmetic or functional defect.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I slipped and put my arm through the glass coffee table, it's a deep cut and it won't stop bleeding."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a recent superficial sharp trauma resulting in a linear skin defect to the left volar forearm occurring approximately 45 minutes ago. The patient has applied a makeshift tourniquet/towel, but bleeding persists. The patient reports a sharp, burning pain at the site. The patient confirms an absence of deep underlying structural damage to tendons, nerves, or major vessels, noting full sensation and movement in all fingers. The patient is anxious regarding the volume of blood loss and the potential need for an emergency department visit.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted by the necessity to apply continuous pressure to the bleeding arm.
Social/Safety Concerns: Glass debris in the living room; high anxiety regarding the injury.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Anxious, pale, holding a blood-soaked towel to the left arm.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the left volar forearm reveals a 6cm superficial linear skin defect exposing the subcutaneous fat layer. Complete haemostasis is achieved with direct pressure during the examination. There is a definitive absence of deep structural damage; active flexion/extension of the wrist and digits is fully intact. The plan involves local anaesthesia infiltration utilizing lidocaine, followed by thorough irrigation and exploration of the wound bed, and primary closure utilizing simple interrupted sutures. Normal neurovascular status is verified distal to the injury (capillary refill <2 seconds, intact sensation). Expected investigation results include normal healing by primary intention without the need for complex diagnostic imaging, as there is no suspicion of a retained radiopaque foreign body.
Environmental Observations: Shattered glass table in the living room; environment otherwise safe.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; unverified tetanus status.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Simple Laceration Repair (Left Volar Forearm) - Status: Stable (Hemostasis achieved)
Differentials:

Tendon Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Full, pain-free active range of motion of the wrist and all digits against resistance, confirming intact flexor tendons.

Arterial Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bleeding is a steady, dark venous ooze rather than a pulsatile, bright red arterial spray, and is easily controlled with localized pressure.

Retained Foreign Body (Glass) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visual exploration of the entire wound base under bright light and local anesthesia confirming a clean tissue bed prior to closure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient sustained an acute, actively bleeding sharp trauma laceration requiring immediate surgical intervention. Telephone triage correctly identified the need for definitive wound closure to arrest the bleeding and prevent infection. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to perform a thorough neurovascular examination to rule out catastrophic limb damage, aggressively irrigate the wound to remove microscopic glass debris, and execute primary closure with sutures. Deferring this procedure until the next in-hours period would have left the patient with an open, bleeding, contaminated wound overnight, significantly elevating the risk of a deep space infection and a poor cosmetic outcome.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Infiltrated 1% Lidocaine with epinephrine locally. Copiously irrigated the wound with 500mL sterile normal saline. Performed primary closure utilizing 5 simple interrupted non-absorbable sutures (4-0 Nylon). Achieved perfect wound edge approximation. Administered a Tetanus toxoid booster (IM deltoid) and applied a sterile protective dressing.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the wound from stretching and the stitches from tearing, strictly commit to wearing a long-sleeved shirt and keeping the affected arm in a resting position (not lifting objects heavier than a coffee cup) for the next 48 hours. By artificially limiting the use of the arm through a clothing cue and a hard weight limit, you actively promote secure primary healing and reduce the risk of wound dehiscence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sterile dressings (provided).
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review strictly in 7 to 10 days for a routine presentation for the planned removal of sutures.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the wound begins bleeding heavily through the thick dressing, if you develop a sudden high fever, or if the arm becomes excessively swollen, hot, and intensely painful.

